---
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import sources from '../data/sources.json';
import provenance from '../data/provenance.json';
import content from '../data/content.json';
const retrieved = new Date(provenance.retrievedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
---
<Shell title="Sources & methodology" active="sources"><article class="prose"><span class="eyebrow">Read the source</span><h1>Sources & methodology</h1><p>{content.methodology}</p><h2>What this app quotes</h2><p>Quran text comes from <a href="https://mcp.quran.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran Foundation’s official Quran MCP <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> and is bundled with the app, so it works without a live connection.</p><ul><li>Arabic: Uthmani Hafs, <code>{provenance.quranEdition}</code>, preserved exactly as retrieved.</li><li>English: Marmaduke Pickthall, <code>{provenance.translationEdition}</code>. His historical public-domain wording is kept rather than silently modernised. A translation is an interpretation of meaning.</li></ul><p>Hadith references name the collection and report number on <a href="https://sunnah.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunnah.com <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> (for example Sahih al-Bukhari 3). Prayer phrases that come from hadith are given in Arabic with our concise English meanings. Reports are summarised in our own words; modern English hadith translations are not republished. Grading labels identify the collection; the app does not regrade chains of narration.</p><p>The Al-Fatiha recording by Adeel Shaikh is bundled locally from Wikimedia Commons under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0. That licence applies to the audio independently of the app code; no endorsement or tajwid certification is implied.</p><h2>Commentary linked</h2><p>Commentary is linked, not republished. Short English notes are our own paraphrases checked against the retrieved Arabic.</p><ul><li>Al-Tafsir al-Muyassar, the concise Arabic commentary prepared by a committee at the King Fahd Quran Printing Complex (<code>ar-muyassar</code>)</li></ul><h2>What is editorial</h2><p>Lesson groupings, practice prompts and the approximate Roman reading aids are editorial. None of it is a ruling or an opinion from quran.ai, Quran.com or Quran Foundation, and no independent scholarly review is claimed.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>This is a meaning study, not a complete prayer manual or a ruling on prayer validity. Valid narrated variants of some phrases exist.</p><h2>Snapshot and reuse</h2><p>Canonical snapshot retrieved on {retrieved}. Source records, checksums and edition names ship with the project. Original application code is MIT licensed. DM Sans, Newsreader and Amiri are hosted locally under the SIL Open Font License, with their licence files included.</p><p class="micro">Snapshot SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.snapshotSha256}</code><br />App content SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.appContentSha256}</code></p><h2>References used in this app</h2><p>{sources.length} linked sources. Each opens the source provider in a new tab.</p><ul class="source-list">{sources.map(source=><li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> <small>{source.kind}{'note' in source && source.note ? ` · ${source.note}` : ''}</small></li>)}</ul><div class="actions top-gap"><a class="button" href="/">Return to the activity <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a> <button class="button secondary" data-print>Print source notes</button></div></article></Shell>
